<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abdeen Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abdeen Palace is a royal palace in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isma-il-pasha">Khedive Ismail</a> and completed in 1874, and the main residence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s rulers until 1952. On 4 February 1942 British tanks surrounded it while Ambassador Miles Lampson forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farouk-of-egypt">King Farouk</a> to appoint a Wafd government under Mustafa el-Nahhas. It is now a museum and presidential palace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abdeen-palace.jpg" alt="Abdeen Palace" width="320" height="128" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was ThutmoseIII at English Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abdeen_Palace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The palace stands on the site of a small mansion owned by Abdeen Bey, after whom it is named. Ismail Pasha built it to replace the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo-citadel">Citadel of Cairo</a>, the seat of Egyptian government since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, as the official government headquarters, and it was also used for state events and ceremonies. Construction began in 1863 and lasted ten years. It was designed by the French architect Léon Rousseau with many Egyptian, Italian, French and other European decorators.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The palace covers 24 feddans and has 500 rooms, adorned with paintings and many clocks, most of them decorated with gold. Joseph Urban added a new wing in 1891, and Sultan Fuad I laid out its garden on another 20 feddans in 1921. Building cost £E700,000, and furnishing a further £E2 million; for four palaces King Fuad spent more than 18 million French francs with a single <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> furniture maker, Linke &amp; Cie. Later rulers spent more on altering and maintaining it. It stands in the Abdeen district, part of the Khedival Cairo area west of Historic Cairo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the 1952 revolution it became one of the presidential palaces. The upper floors, once the royal family's living quarters, are kept for visiting foreign dignitaries, while the lower floors hold the Silver Museum, the Arms Museum, the Royal Family Museum and the Presidential Gifts Museum. The Historical Documents Museum opened in January 2005; among its papers are the Ottoman firman that established the rule of Muhammad Ali and his family and a certificate of the Order of the Iron Crown of the Kingdom of Araucanía and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abdeen Palace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1942 British tanks surrounded <b>Abdeen Palace</b> to force Farouk to appoint a Wafd government, and the Axis advance was halted at El Alamein.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-egypt">Kingdom of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
